<div class="sop-meta">
  <div><strong>Trigger</strong> Calendar invite accepted from phase 01</div>
  <div><strong>Owner</strong> Whoever owns the lead</div>
  <div><strong>Time</strong> 20 min on the call + 15 min notes after</div>
  <div><strong>Output</strong> Decision: proposal, follow-up, or polite decline</div>
</div>

<h2>Before the call (5 min prep)</h2>
<ul class="sop-checklist">
  <li>Open their current website (if any). Note the stack via <code>view-source</code> or wappalyzer (WordPress / Squarespace / Wix / etc.).</li>
  <li>Run their homepage through PageSpeed Insights. Note the mobile score.</li>
  <li>Open their Google Business Profile. Check review count, last activity, photos.</li>
  <li>Glance at their top 2-3 competitors in the same metro. What does the field look like?</li>
  <li>Have <code>/what-we-build/</code> and <code>/pricing/</code> open in tabs in case they ask.</li>
</ul>

<h2>The call structure (20 min)</h2>

<h3>Minutes 0-2: Connection + framing</h3>
<p>"Thanks for the time. The next 20 minutes are mostly me asking questions. No pitch deck, no slides. End of the call I will either book a follow-up to start the build, or I'll be honest if I am not the right fit. Sound good?"</p>

<h3>Minutes 2-10: Listen first</h3>
<p>Open with: <em>"Tell me about the business. How long, what you do, who your customers are."</em> Let them talk. Don't interrupt unless they ramble past 4 minutes.</p>
<p>Then the qualifying questions, in this order:</p>
<ol>
  <li>What's the current website situation? (None / WordPress they hate / Squarespace / Wix / "I built it myself")</li>
  <li>What's broken about the current site? (If they have one.) "Doesn't bring leads," "looks dated," "I can't update it," "speed."</li>
  <li>What does a good month of leads look like for you? Set a baseline.</li>
  <li>Where do most leads come from today? (Google search, GBP, referrals, paid ads.) Helps us know what NOT to break at launch.</li>
  <li>Who handles the website now? (Them, an agency, a nephew, nobody.) Tells us about handoff complexity.</li>
  <li>What's your rough budget thinking? Not a binding number; I want to know if I am in the same universe.</li>
  <li>Any specific features they already know they want? (Booking, online quotes, blog, multiple service-area pages.)</li>
  <li>Timeline pressure? (Wedding-season deadline, mid-summer demand, "yesterday.")</li>
</ol>

<h3>Minutes 10-15: Frame the deliverable</h3>
<p>Walk them through what we'd build, in 3-4 sentences max. Reference their specific situation. Don't read the deliverable list verbatim.</p>
<p><strong>Example reframe:</strong> "Based on what you described, what I'd build is a 7-page custom site: home, about, the four services you mentioned, a service-area page covering Fort Collins + Loveland + Greeley, and contact. Plus the operational pages every site needs. Built in 4 weeks from when we get your photos. Hosted by us, no WordPress, $175/month flat. Lights stay on, content updates whenever you want them, no surprise fees."</p>

<h3>Minutes 15-19: Their questions</h3>
<p>Common questions to be ready for: pricing details, who owns the code, what happens if I leave, do you do SEO, can I edit it myself.</p>

<h3>Minute 19-20: Clear next step</h3>
<p>One of three:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>"This sounds right. Let's do it."</strong> Send the proposal + agreement same day. Schedule the kickoff call for next week.</li>
  <li><strong>"Let me think about it / talk to my partner."</strong> Send proposal anyway with a 14-day soft-hold. Don't chase.</li>
  <li><strong>"This isn't quite what I had in mind."</strong> Probe gently for the mismatch. If genuinely not a fit, say so directly: "I don't think I am the right people for this; here's who I'd point you toward."</li>
</ul>

<h2>Qualifying questions: red flags + green flags</h2>

<div class="sop-callout sop-callout--ok">
  <strong>Green flags</strong>
  Owner-led, knows their customer well, has a real backlog of work, doesn't have a website OR has one they actively dislike, accepts $175/mo without flinching, asks specific questions about deliverables not pricing.
</div>

<div class="sop-callout sop-callout--warn">
  <strong>Yellow flags</strong>
  Multiple decision-makers ("I'll need to check with my partner"), price-sensitive on a 20-minute call, hasn't thought about what they actually want yet. Send a proposal but don't expect fast close.
</div>

<div class="sop-callout sop-callout--warn">
  <strong>Red flags</strong>
  Wants 20+ pages on a $175/mo plan, expects daily back-and-forth on design, treats the call like a vendor pitch ("convince me"), refuses to share their current site, asks about ranking guarantees, wants us to also run their Google Ads, dropships product. Decline politely.
</div>

<h2>Post-call notes (15 min)</h2>
<p>Within 30 minutes of the call ending, write notes in CRM. Use this template:</p>

<div class="sop-template">
  <div class="sop-template-label">Discovery notes template</div>
<pre>Business: {name}
Owner: {first last}
Industry: {trade}
Service area: {cities}
Current site: {url or "none"}, {stack: WP / Squarespace / etc.}
Current site issues: {list of complaints}
Lead volume now: {monthly leads or "unsure"}
Lead sources: {GBP / search / referrals / ads}
Budget signal: {OK / borderline / out of range}
Features wanted: {bullets}
Timeline: {weeks / months / "no rush"}
Fit: {strong / moderate / decline}
Next step: {proposal sent / 90-day follow-up / decline / referred to X}
Notes: {any color, the why-they-called-now story}</pre>
</div>

<h2>Common issues</h2>

<div class="sop-callout sop-callout--warn">
  <strong>They want a quote on the call</strong>
  Resist. The 20-minute call is for understanding the build, not committing to a number. "I'll have a written proposal in your inbox by end of day; the call gives me what I need to scope it correctly."
</div>

<div class="sop-callout sop-callout--warn">
  <strong>They want to skip the discovery call entirely</strong>
  Decline politely. "I do not quote without the call. If 20 minutes is too much, I am probably not a fit." Closes higher than caving.
</div>

<div class="sop-callout">
  <strong>The call runs long</strong>
  At 25 minutes, name it: "I am past the 20. Want to go another 10 or wrap up and continue by email?" Most pick the wrap.
</div>

<h2>Exit criteria</h2>
<p>Notes logged in CRM, decision made (proposal / follow-up / decline). If proposal: <a href="/sop/proposal-agreement/">phase 03</a> kicks off same day.</p>
